WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.600
The natural world is full of extraordinary animals with amazing life histories.

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:15.720
Yet certain stories are more intriguing than most.

00:00:15.720 --> 00:00:22.960
The mysteries of a butterfly's life cycle or the strange biology of the emperor penguin.

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:30.360
Some of these creatures were surrounded by myth and misunderstandings for a very long time.

00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:34.960
And some have only recently revealed their secrets.

00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:38.560
These are the animals that stand out from the crowd.

00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:51.160
The curiosities I find particularly fascinating.

00:00:51.160 --> 00:00:56.960
Some animals have intriguing ways of protecting their skin.

00:00:56.960 --> 00:01:03.960
The hippopotamus lives in Africa under the hot tropical sun, yet doesn't get sunburnt.

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:10.760
And capuchin monkeys live in insect-infested jungles, but hardly ever get bitten.

00:01:10.760 --> 00:01:23.960
How do these animals beat the elements and protect themselves from sun, parasites and disease?

00:01:23.960 --> 00:01:28.760
Hippos are large land mammals that can weigh up to three tons.

00:01:28.760 --> 00:01:32.960
And they need to keep their huge bodies cool and protected from the sun.

00:01:32.960 --> 00:01:39.680
To avoid the heat, they spend much of the day swimming, as they are doing now in the waters behind me.

00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:44.960
But when they are on land, strangely, they don't appear to get sunburnt.

00:01:44.960 --> 00:01:49.360
The secret of their sun tolerance lies within their skin.

00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:52.560
It can sometimes appear shiny and greasy.

00:01:52.560 --> 00:02:03.560
It has unique properties that shocked the early explorers and now excites modern scientists.

00:02:03.560 --> 00:02:10.360
Hippos live in Africa south of the Sahara, where temperatures can reach 40 degrees centigrade.

00:02:10.360 --> 00:02:21.760
But they spend much of the day submerged in rivers, lakes and swamps, and so avoid the worst of the sun's rays.

00:02:21.760 --> 00:02:29.360
They possess formidable teeth, but they are in fact herbivores and eat mostly grass, great quantities of it.

00:02:29.360 --> 00:02:33.760
And they graze mostly at night.

00:02:33.760 --> 00:02:44.160
Even so, continually moving in and out of water, together with being roasted by the rays of the sun, could be very damaging to their skin.

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:51.960
But curiously, hippos remain healthy.

00:02:51.960 --> 00:02:57.560
Throughout history, the hippopotamus has been the subject of many strange tales.

00:02:57.560 --> 00:03:05.760
The Greeks claimed they sweated blood, and the Romans said they deliberately pierced their skin on sharp rushes to release blood.

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:12.160
It seemed bizarre that an animal would make itself bleed on purpose.

00:03:12.160 --> 00:03:22.360
In the 19th century, one special hippopotamus allowed people to get a closer look at these strange skin secretions.

00:03:22.360 --> 00:03:28.360
In 1849, the British consul for Egypt, Charles Augustus Murray,

00:03:28.360 --> 00:03:36.160
formally requested that the Pasha of Egypt help capture a hippo for the Zoological Society of London.

00:03:36.160 --> 00:03:46.360
Hunters searched the reeds on a remote island called Obesh, 2,000 kilometres up the Nile from Cairo.

00:03:46.360 --> 00:03:50.760
They discovered a male hippo that was only a few days old.

00:03:50.760 --> 00:03:53.960
When they tried to grab it, a strange thing happened.

00:03:53.960 --> 00:04:02.360
Murray describes how a slimy exudation lavishly poured forth from the innumerable pores in the skin,

00:04:02.360 --> 00:04:07.360
rendering it so slippery that the animal was impossible to hold.

00:04:07.360 --> 00:04:16.360
The hunters dropped the baby hippo back into the waters of the Nile, but they managed to retrieve it again using the hook of a spear.

00:04:16.360 --> 00:04:23.160
The price hippo was named Obesh after the island of its capture, and here he is.

00:04:27.560 --> 00:04:31.760
Before his capture, young Obesh lived with his mother.

00:04:31.760 --> 00:04:39.560
She had moved away from the herd to give birth alone, and she protected him from lions and crocodiles.

00:04:39.560 --> 00:04:43.960
Whether Obesh became accidentally separated from his mother, we'll never know,

00:04:43.960 --> 00:04:51.960
but we do know that secretions from his skin made him so slippery that he very nearly escaped capture.

00:04:51.960 --> 00:04:55.560
At this time, very little was known about hippos,

00:04:55.560 --> 00:05:02.960
and many people believed that they were some kind of horse that had taken to living in rivers.

00:05:02.960 --> 00:05:10.360
In the early 20th century, naturalists decided that they were closely related to pigs.

00:05:10.360 --> 00:05:21.960
DNA evidence, however, now shows that, in fact, their ancestors were cetaceans, the group that contains whales and dolphins.

00:05:21.960 --> 00:05:27.960
So hippos still retain many adaptations for a life in water.

00:05:31.360 --> 00:05:38.160
Hippos are very heavy animals, but for most of their time, their bodies are supported by water.

00:05:38.160 --> 00:05:44.560
They're not really very good swimmers. In the water, they move by bounding across the bottom.

00:05:44.560 --> 00:05:52.960
They're well adapted to a semi-aquatic life because their ears, their eyes and their nostrils are all towards the top of the head,

00:05:52.960 --> 00:06:00.760
which enables them to lie almost totally submerged and yet still keep notice of what's going on on land.

00:06:00.760 --> 00:06:08.360
But their skin is almost entirely hairless, so on land, it has to be kept moist.

00:06:11.360 --> 00:06:15.960
In order to prevent young Obesh from sunburn and drying out,

00:06:15.960 --> 00:06:25.360
the Egyptian pasha had a boat built with a bathing pool to transport Obesh in comfort all the way down the River Nile.

00:06:25.360 --> 00:06:32.960
Accompanied by several cows to supply him with milk, he arrived safely in Cairo four months later.

00:06:34.560 --> 00:06:40.760
On receiving Obesh, the British Consul wrote excitedly to the Zoological Society of London,

00:06:40.760 --> 00:06:46.360
confirming that the hippo was alive and as tame and playful as a puppy.

00:06:46.360 --> 00:06:49.960
But his travels were not yet over.

00:06:49.960 --> 00:06:57.560
In the spring of 1850, Obesh was taken to Alexandria to board a P&amp;O steamship called the Rippon.

00:06:57.560 --> 00:07:05.560
A special hippo house with a water tank was built on the deck and in May, Obesh arrived safely in Southampton.

00:07:05.560 --> 00:07:10.560
With the help of Block and Tackle, he was loaded onto a train bound for London.

00:07:10.560 --> 00:07:17.360
And at 10 o'clock at night, the tired hippo and his keeper reached London Zoo.

00:07:17.360 --> 00:07:22.960
His home was a newly constructed enclosure complete with a heated swimming pool.

00:07:22.960 --> 00:07:27.760
After many hours of travelling, the hippo gratefully plunged into the water.

00:07:27.760 --> 00:07:31.560
Obesh, the hippo sensation, had arrived.

00:07:31.560 --> 00:07:46.560
A journey of over 5,000 miles by sailboat, steamboat and a train brought a hippo to England, the first one since Roman times.

00:07:46.560 --> 00:07:57.560
Now Europeans had a chance to get close to this unusual creature and perhaps learn more about its strange skin secretions.

00:07:57.560 --> 00:08:04.560
Later, more hippos arrived at other zoos and the blood red sweat was seen again.

00:08:04.560 --> 00:08:09.560
In the cooler climate of Europe, hippos don't sweat very much.

00:08:09.560 --> 00:08:17.560
But zookeepers have reported that sometimes in the morning they see red trickles forming on the flanks of these animals.

00:08:17.560 --> 00:08:29.560
It comes from particularly large pores which form streaks on the animal's side which does look a little like blood.

00:08:29.560 --> 00:08:38.560
We've known for some time that this is a moisturiser but why it's red has only just been discovered.

00:08:38.560 --> 00:08:44.560
Here's a more. Come on. There we go.

00:08:44.560 --> 00:08:56.560
Recently, Japanese scientists were intrigued to see photos of a wild baby hippo with light pink skin that still didn't burn under the harsh African sun.

00:08:56.560 --> 00:09:02.560
They wondered if the red secretion played a role in protecting its pale skin.

00:09:02.560 --> 00:09:07.560
So they collected hippo secretion from captive hippos to look at its composition.

00:09:07.560 --> 00:09:19.560
They discovered two pigments, a red one that they named hippo sudoric acid and an orange one that they called nor hippo sudoric acid.

00:09:19.560 --> 00:09:29.560
The red pigment was found to absorb harmful wavelengths of light and both pigments were antibacterial.

00:09:29.560 --> 00:09:40.560
Here was the answer to why hippos never got sunburned and why the wounds of battling males rarely became infected.

00:09:40.560 --> 00:09:52.560
The mysterious slime is neither blood nor sweat but a specialised secretion that turns red in sunlight and protects the hippo skin.

00:09:52.560 --> 00:10:01.560
So what became of Obeish, the first ever hippo in captivity, that gave us a close up view of these curious creatures?

00:10:01.560 --> 00:10:12.560
For several years he was a sensation of the London Zoo. He even inspired the hippo polka, a popular dance of its time.

00:10:12.560 --> 00:10:23.560
But visitors grew weary of him. Some were disappointed not to see a giant river horse and others expected a ferocious beast, not a gentle giant.

00:10:23.560 --> 00:10:37.560
Obeish died in 1878 at the age of 28 and he and others that followed taught us some intriguing things about hippos, including the reason for the blood red droplets found on their skin.

00:10:37.560 --> 00:10:47.560
So hippos can produce their very own natural sun cream that is waterproof, moisturising and antibacterial.

00:10:47.560 --> 00:11:11.560
Next meet another animal that also has its own curious cure, the Capuchin monkey that deals with biting insects in a particularly intriguing way.

00:11:11.560 --> 00:11:18.560
The hippo produces a natural sun cream to prevent its skin from getting burnt.

00:11:18.560 --> 00:11:34.560
Capuchin monkeys also need to protect their skin and do so in a very surprising way.

00:11:34.560 --> 00:11:45.560
When early explorers reached the Americas in the 15th century, they encountered small monkeys with patches of dark brown fur on their heads that resembled hoods.

00:11:45.560 --> 00:11:55.560
So they named them after a group of Franciscan friars called Capuchin monks.

00:11:55.560 --> 00:12:07.560
Capuchin monkeys quickly charmed their way into our hearts with dexterous hands and inquisitive personalities, they seemed very human like.

00:12:07.560 --> 00:12:19.560
They were also adept at learning tricks and soon became popular performers on our streets.

00:12:19.560 --> 00:12:26.560
In the past we used to teach monkeys how to do things, how to perform tricks.

00:12:26.560 --> 00:12:33.560
But things are different today. Today monkeys are teaching us things.

00:12:33.560 --> 00:12:52.560
Watch what happens when I give them a few spring onions and some chilli peppers.

00:13:03.560 --> 00:13:19.560
They're clearly not eating what I offer them, they're rubbing themselves with the peppers and the onions.

00:13:19.560 --> 00:13:45.560
You might think that that's because they're captive monkeys and they are just doing that to entertain themselves, but not so. I've seen Capuchins do just that in the wild.

00:13:45.560 --> 00:13:58.560
These white-faced Capuchins in Costa Rica reacted in much the same way when they came across a particular rainforest plant, the piper plant.

00:13:58.560 --> 00:14:14.560
These leaves have a distinctive licorice scent and they're hard to come by, so when they do find them the monkeys pass the leaves around the troop so that everyone can have a share.

00:14:14.560 --> 00:14:31.560
Both in the wild and in captivity, Capuchin monkeys become similarly excited at the sight of lemons or limes, and again the same frenzied activity and fur rubbing follows.

00:14:31.560 --> 00:14:39.560
What is it about these plants that gets the monkeys so excited?

00:14:39.560 --> 00:14:48.560
We know they all give off a pungent smell, so could this be what the Capuchins are after.

00:14:48.560 --> 00:14:57.560
Smell plays an important part in the lives of many primates, but none more so than in the lives of these lovely ring-tailed lemurs.

00:14:57.560 --> 00:15:06.560
They use it both to establish their position within the troop and also the boundaries, the frontiers of their territory.

00:15:06.560 --> 00:15:17.560
If you look at the inside of their forearms, there's a black patch without fur, and there the skin is loaded with glands that produce a very strong smell.

00:15:17.560 --> 00:15:28.560
And when these boys go into battle, whoops, when they go into battle, they draw their furry tail through their forearms, loading it with scent from those glands,

00:15:28.560 --> 00:15:36.560
and then they wave it over their backs in the direction of their enemies in a kind of stink fight.

00:15:36.560 --> 00:15:39.560
You understand that, don't you?

00:15:39.560 --> 00:15:44.560
Oh!

00:15:44.560 --> 00:15:49.560
The pungent scent is also used by males during the mating season.

00:15:49.560 --> 00:16:01.560
This male has rubbed his own distinctive smell onto his tail, and he now wafts it towards a female to signal his intentions.

00:16:01.560 --> 00:16:07.560
But she is not entirely convinced.

00:16:07.560 --> 00:16:17.560
Unlike lemurs, Capuchins don't have scent glands, so some thought that they could be using the smell of certain plants for communication.

00:16:17.560 --> 00:16:27.560
But it turns out that they have a different perfume for that job, urine, which they apply lavishly to their fur.

00:16:27.560 --> 00:16:32.560
So why, then, do they also anoint themselves with other smells?

00:16:32.560 --> 00:16:37.560
The answer may be found in our own history.

00:16:37.560 --> 00:16:50.560
The early Romans noticed some 2,000 years ago that the fruits and leaves of the lemon plant have an exceptionally strong scent that can be used to ward off insects.

00:16:50.560 --> 00:16:59.560
A further clue as to why Capuchins might cover themselves in such pungent smells comes from this plant, the piper plant.

00:16:59.560 --> 00:17:05.560
Throughout the Amazon, Indian tribes apply it as an antiseptic on wounds.

00:17:05.560 --> 00:17:10.560
And in Costa Rica, it's used as an insect repellent.

00:17:10.560 --> 00:17:22.560
Could it be that Capuchin monkeys protect themselves against the onslaught of mosquitoes in much the same way as humans do, by rubbing themselves with mosquito repellent?

00:17:26.560 --> 00:17:34.560
In 1993, scientists at Oxford University decided to put the question to the test.

00:17:34.560 --> 00:17:41.560
They collected some feather lice and put them into petri dishes overnight.

00:17:41.560 --> 00:17:49.560
Into one dish, they also placed a slice of lime.

00:17:49.560 --> 00:17:54.560
The next day, the lice without the lime were mostly alive.

00:17:54.560 --> 00:18:02.560
Whilst in the other dish, two-thirds had died and the remainder were paralysed.

00:18:02.560 --> 00:18:08.560
Clearly, the lime contains a lethal insecticide.

00:18:08.560 --> 00:18:22.560
Today, we know that citrus fruit peel does indeed contain insecticides, which disrupt the nervous system of many small insects, causing them to become uncoordinated and paralysed.

00:18:22.560 --> 00:18:30.560
The leaves of the piper plant are antiseptic and contain substances that protect against fungal and bacterial infection.

00:18:30.560 --> 00:18:41.560
The lime and chilli pepper extract is commonly used in households and gardens to deter small mammals and insect pests.

00:18:41.560 --> 00:18:48.560
So it seems that the clever monkeys know exactly how to make the best use of nature's remedies.

00:18:48.560 --> 00:18:59.560
Recent research has also revealed that Capuchins anoint themselves far more during the wet season, when mosquitoes are more abundant and the risk of infection is higher.

00:18:59.560 --> 00:19:06.560
Troops use different plants, possibly simply because they have to use what's locally available.

00:19:06.560 --> 00:19:17.560
But there's one substance with insect repellent qualities that appeals to primates, including Capuchins, that comes not from a plant, but from an animal.

00:19:17.560 --> 00:19:21.560
An animal like this.

00:19:21.560 --> 00:19:23.560
A giant millipede.

00:19:23.560 --> 00:19:31.560
When attacked or in danger, tropical millipedes often produce a powerful defensive secretion.

00:19:31.560 --> 00:19:37.560
And black lemurs have worked out how to use this to their advantage.

00:19:37.560 --> 00:19:46.560
When they find a millipede, they give it a gentle bite to the head to make it release its secretion and then rub this through their fur.

00:19:46.560 --> 00:19:56.560
The toxic fluid has a strong smell and is highly irritating, but it protects the lemurs against mosquitoes.

00:19:56.560 --> 00:20:01.560
This pungent secretion has apparently another strange effect.

00:20:01.560 --> 00:20:07.560
It seems to act as a narcotic, sending the lemur into a kind of trance.

00:20:07.560 --> 00:20:13.560
Like other drugs, it has powerful side effects.

00:20:13.560 --> 00:20:20.560
We still don't understand how Capuchins and lemurs select the plants that they use for medicinal purposes.

00:20:20.560 --> 00:20:31.560
These Capuchins behind me were born and raised in captivity, so they never encountered the plants that their parents and ancestors would have used.

00:20:31.560 --> 00:20:35.560
So how do the monkeys know which plants to choose?

00:20:35.560 --> 00:20:41.560
Can they detect particular substances in them, or is it something they learn from others?

00:20:41.560 --> 00:20:50.560
We don't yet know the answers, but it could be that babies learn by watching the adults and that it's passed down the family line.

00:20:50.560 --> 00:20:57.560
It's clearly a great social event with everyone joining in.

00:20:57.560 --> 00:21:06.560
And afterwards, the entire group appears to be more tightly bonded.

00:21:06.560 --> 00:21:15.560
When Europeans first saw monkeys in the wild, they thought that they were imitating what people did in some of their behaviours.

00:21:15.560 --> 00:21:25.560
But quite the reverse. It now turns out that many of the local people did things that the monkeys had taught them, using plants as medicines.

00:21:25.560 --> 00:21:36.560
So it seems clever monkeys have taught us a trick or two.

00:21:36.560 --> 00:21:42.560
So it's clear that we're not alone in using medicines against injuries and infections.

00:21:42.560 --> 00:22:10.560
Both Capuchin monkeys and hippos discovered some medical remedies long before we did.

00:22:10.560 --> 00:22:20.560
Thanks for watching!
